    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d42">

                <pb facs="0290" n="32" sameAs="frus:frus1915Supp/pg32"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72/2541</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Page</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram—Extract]<anchor n="1" xml:id="d42-f1"/>
                    </seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-05-08T17:00:00+00:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">May 8, 1915, 5 p. m.</hi>
                        </date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received 10.30 p. m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>2068. The foreign editor of the <hi rendition="#italic">Times</hi>, a usually
                    well-informed and trustworthy man, who knows all the principal European
                    statesmen, is just returned from a week in France. He tells me in strictest
                    confidence that England, France, and Russia made a bargain with Italy on April
                    30, agreeing to cede to Italy very large parts of Austrian territory, some of
                    which has a Slavic population, if Italy comes into the war within a month. This
                    was done without consulting Servia and against her wishes. Italy will soon come
                    in if she keeps her agreement, to be followed by Roumania. I have heard
                    unofficial confirmation of this agreement here. The same editor informs me that
                    General Joffre told him that he is confident that he would break through the
                    German lines within a month....</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Ambassador</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
